'''Yinchorr''' was an [[Expansion Region]] location that housed one of the [[Galactic Empire]]'s [[Yinchorr Training Facility|training hubs]] for the [[Emperor's Royal Guard]]. In [[3 ABY]], it received an urgent transfer request for the [[capital]], [[Coruscant]], following a mass [[Assassination of the Emperor's Royal Guard|assassination]] of Royal Guards stationed there. Yinchorr's control was [[Siege at Yinchorr|contested]] by the [[New Republic]] in [[4 ABY]], leading to a siege situation. The location ended up in New Republic territory by [[9 ABY]].

==Description==
Yinchorr was a location<ref name="Rise" /> in the [[Yinchorr system]] within the [[Expansion Region]]<ref name="Systems" /> and [[the Interior]] regions.<ref name="Interior" /> It was situated in the [[Fellwe sector]] at the coordinates L-8 on the [[Standard Galactic Grid]].<ref name="Systems" /> Yinchorr shared its name with the [[Sentience|sentient]] [[Reptile|reptilian]] [[species]] known as the [[Yinchorri]].<ref name="Living Force">''[[The Living Force]]''</ref>

==History==
{{Quote|The Archivist suggests that one aspect of this was a mass assassination by poison of the Imperial Royal Guard serving at the Imperial Palace on Coruscant. How this would have been possible is hard to imagine, as these individuals must have been some of the most carefully isolated and vetted people in the galaxy. However, there is also a record in Imperial databases requesting additional personnel be transferred to Coruscant from Yinchorr&mdash;one of the training hubs for the Royal Guard&mdash;so while implausible it is possible there is some underlying truth to the claim.|Beaumont Kin, ''The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire''|<ref name="Rise" />}}
[[File:AssassinationPalpatinesRoyalGuard-CrimsonReign2.jpg|thumb|left|300px|Yinchorr received an urgent transfer order following a mass assassination of Royal Guards on Coruscant (pictured).]]
During the [[Imperial Era|time]] of the [[Galactic Empire]], Yinchorr housed one of the [[Yinchorr Training Facility|training hubs]] for the [[Galactic Emperor|Emperor]] [[Darth Sidious|Palpatine's]] [[Emperor's Royal Guard|Royal Guard]].<ref name="Rise" /> In [[3 ABY]],<ref name="Date">{{OutlawsComicDate|Crimson Reign 2|''Crimson Reign'' 2}}</ref> following the [[Crimson Dawn]] [[syndicate]]'s [[Assassination of the Emperor's Royal Guard|mass assassination]] of the Royal Guard<ref name="Crimson Reign 2">[[Crimson Reign 2|''Crimson Reign'' 2]]</ref> stationed at the [[Imperial Palace]] on the [[Galaxy|galactic]] [[capital]] [[Coruscant]], an urgent order was made for additional personnel to be transferred from the Yinchorr Training Facility to Coruscant. It was noted in the file "Urgent transfer order #XJ8177," which was kept in the "Yinchorr Training Facility" section of the [[Hall of Imperial Register|Imperial Archives]].<ref name="Rise" />

In [[4 ABY]],<ref name="Date 2">{{AlphabetSquadronDate}}</ref> during the [[Galactic Civil War]] between the Empire and the recently [[Declaration of a New Republic|reorganized]] [[New Republic]], the latter faction [[Siege at Yinchorr|fought]] to take Yinchorr from the Empire, the conflict devolving in to a siege situation. The matters at Yinchorr were noted in a briefing on the progress of the war that was read by New Republic [[General]] [[Hera Syndulla]].<ref name="Alphabet Squadron">[[Alphabet Squadron (novel)|''Alphabet Squadron'']]</ref> The war [[Galactic Concordance|ended]] in the Empire's fall in [[5 ABY]],<ref name="Timelines">''[[Star Wars: Timelines]]''</ref> and Yinchorr was within New Republic territory<ref name="MandoVG" /> in [[9 ABY]].<ref name="9 ABY">{{MandoVGDate}}</ref> The file on Urgent transfer order #XJ8177 was eventually uncovered by the [[historian]] [[Beaumont Kin]], who wrote about Crimson Dawn's [[War against the Sith|efforts]] against the Empire in<ref name="Rise" /> his<ref name="TROSVD">''[[Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker: The Visual Dictionary]]''</ref> [[35 ABY]]<ref name="35 ABY">{{RiseAndFallDate}}</ref> [[book]] ''[[The Rise and Fall of the Galactic Empire]]''. While he was doubtful of claims about the assassination of the Royal Guard, Kin noted that the transfer order gave some support to it.<ref name="Rise" />

==Behind the scenes==
[[File:The_squall.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Yinchorr in the ''Star Wars'' Legends continuity]]
Yinchorr was introduced into the current ''[[Star Wars]]'' [[canon]] when it was included as a partially labeled map point in the [[Star Wars: Build the Millennium Falcon 52|fifty-second issue]] of [[De Agostini]]'s magazine series ''[[Star Wars: Build the Millennium Falcon]]'',<ref name="BMF 52">{{BuildFalconCite|52|Guide to the Galaxy}}</ref> written by [[Mark Newbold]]<ref name="Newbold">{{DMCite|dm=File:MarkNewbold-CreditsBMF.png|creator=[[Mark Newbold]]}}</ref> and published around [[December 30]], [[2015]].<ref name="BMFdate">{{BuildFalconDates|52}}</ref> The location was later named in full in a map point in the [[2016]] [[Fantasy Flight Games]] sourcebook [[Star Wars: The Force Awakens Beginner Game|''Star Wars: The Force Awakens'' Beginner Game]].<ref name="TFABG">[[Star Wars: The Force Awakens Beginner Game|''Star Wars: The Force Awakens'' Beginner Game]] &mdash; Based on corresponding data for the [[Yinchorr system]]</ref> Yinchorr was first described as a distinct location in the [[2019]] novel [[Alphabet Squadron (novel)|''Alphabet Squadron'']], the first novel in [[Alexander Freed]]'s ''[[Star Wars: Alphabet Squadron]]'' trilogy.<ref name="Alphabet Squadron" /> The [[Planet/Legends|planet]] [[Yinchorr/Legends|Yinchorr]] originated in [[Star Wars Legends|''Star Wars'' Legends]] continuity, where it first appeared in the [[Star Wars (1977) 86|eighty-sixth issue]] of the [[1977]] [[Star Wars (1977)|''Star Wars'' comic run]], written by [[Randy Stradley]], penciled by [[Bob McLeod]],<ref name="SW86">[[Star Wars (1977) 86|''Star Wars'' (1977) 86]]</ref> and published by [[Marvel Comics]] on [[May 15]], [[1984]].<ref name="Marvel SW86">{{Marvel|url=comics/issue/53876/star_wars_1977_86|text=Star Wars (1977) #86|nolive=1}}</ref>

